(docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #111. `For each x in outer` binding x to a nested
 list yields a copy - one of the four forms the ruling names by name.
 The analyzer does not (yet, independent of this brief) accept a for-each
 loop variable as a direct `Set element N of`/`append ... to` target, so
 this isolates the binding itself instead: aliasing x into `snapshot` via
 a plain `Set` (untouched by #111 - plain variable assignment stays a
 reference, only placement INSIDE a collection copies) and mutating
 through that alias. If the loop variable had been bound to `a1`'s own
 block, `a1` would show the mutation too.)

a list called a1 is [1, 2].
a list called outer is [a1].
a list called snapshot is [].
For each x in outer,
    Set snapshot to x.
Set element 1 of snapshot to 999.
print outer.
print a1.
print snapshot.
